We will start with **direct substitution** (step A):

$\begin{align}
h(\blueD{4})&=\dfrac{(\blueD{4})-4}{\sqrt{3(\blueD{4})-3}-3}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{0}{\sqrt{9}-3}
\\\\
&=\dfrac00
\end{align}$

We got the **indeterminate form** $\dfrac00$ (step D).

Now we need to determine which analytical method is applicable.

Since we have a quotient with a square root, we will use the method of rationalization by **conjugates** (step F):

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{x-4}{\sqrt{3x-3}-3}&=\dfrac{x-4}{\sqrt{3x-3}-3}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt{3x-3}+3}{\sqrt{3x-3}+3}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{(x-4)({\sqrt{3x-3}+3})}{3x-3-9}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{(x-4)({\sqrt{3x-3}+3})}{3x-12}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{(x-4)}({\sqrt{3x-3}+3})}{3\cancel{(x-4)}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\sqrt{3x-3}+3}{3}
\end{align}$

Now we can try **direct substitution** (step A) once again!

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{\sqrt{3(\blueD{4})-3}+3}{3}&=\dfrac{\sqrt{9}+3}{3}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{3+3}{3}
\\\\
&=2
\end{align}$

We got a number, so we've **found the limit** (step C)!

This means $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 4}h(x)=2$.
